## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design realistic cross-service end-to-end tests with real-time updates, failure modes, and proper data isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time event streams (WebSocket/SSE) and state machine validation
- Test isolation with per-tenant data and fresh environments
- Deterministic inputs via seed data and mocked external services
- Idempotency and eventual consistency verification across services
- Failure injection testing (disconnects, latency, partial outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch using Playwright
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test.describe('E2E Food Delivery Flow', () => {
  let wsServer, testData;

  test.beforeAll(async () => {
    wsServer = await startMockWebSocketServer();
    testData = await seedDeterministicTestData();
  });

  test('complete delivery flow with driver tracking', async ({ page }) => {
    const driverUpdates = [];

    // Simulate real-time driver location stream
    wsServer.on('driver-location', (data) => driverUpdates.push(data));

    // 1. Place order
    await page.goto('/order');
    await page.fill('#address', testData.address);
    await page.click('#place-order');
    const orderId = await expectOrderConfirmed(page);

    // 2. Restaurant accepts
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'accepted' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'accepted');

    // 3. Driver assigned
    await wsServer.emit('driver-assigned', { orderId, driver: testData.driver });
    await expectDriverAssigned(page, testData.driver);

    // 4. Verify location/ETA streaming
    await wsServer.emit('driver-location', { lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060, eta: 10 });
    await expectETA(page, '10 min');

    // 5. Payment processed
    await stubPaymentAPI({ orderId, status: 'success' });
    await expectPaymentStatus(page, 'paid');

    // 6. Delivery completes
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'delivered');

    // Verify idempotent event handling
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    expect(driverUpdates).toHaveLength(1); // No duplicate processing

    // Verify eventual consistency
    await expectExternalServiceSynced(orderId);
  });

  test('handles driver disconnect gracefully', async ({ page }) => {
    const orderId = await placeOrder(page);
    await wsServer.simulateDisconnect();

    await expectReconnectionLogic(page);
    await expectDriverReassigned(page);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Feature-flag testing in multi-tenant, privacy-focused applications is common at scale. This question evaluates understanding of determinism, data isolation, and resilience under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant test isolation
- Feature-flag gating and consent API mocking
- Tenant-scoped headers and data boundaries
- Resilience: 503 handling with exponential backoff
- Desktop and mobile parity in UI and data latency

## Code Example

```javascript
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('privacy flag rollout across tenants', async ({ page }) => {
  // Seed tenants A (flag on) and B (flag off)
  const tenantA = await seedTenant({ dataSharingEnabled: true });
  const tenantB = await seedTenant({ dataSharingEnabled: false });

  // Mock feature-flag and consent services
  await page.route('**/api/feature-flags', (route) => {
    const tenantId = route.request().headers()['x-tenant-id'];
    route.fulfill({
      status: 200,
      contentType: 'application/json',
      body: JSON.stringify({
        dataSharing: tenantId === tenantA.id
      })
    });
  });

  await page.route('**/api/consent', (route) => {
    route.fulfill({ status: 200, contentType: 'application/json', body: JSON.stringify({ granted: true }) });
  });

  // Verify tenant A sees dashboard
  await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantA.id } });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-content"]')).toBeVisible();

  // Verify tenant B is blocked
  await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantB.id } });
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-content"]')).not.toBeVisible();

  // Validate tenant-scoped headers in API calls
  const apiRequest = page.waitForRequest('**/api/analytics/**');
  await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantA.id } });
  expect((await apiRequest).headers()['x-tenant-id']).toBe(tenantA.id);

  // Test 503 retry with exponential backoff
  let attempts = 0;
  await page.route('**/api/analytics/**', (route) => {
    attempts++;
    if (attempts < 3) {
      route.fulfill({ status: 503 });
    } else {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ latency: 120 }) });
    }
  });

  // Desktop and mobile parity
  for (const viewport of [{ width: 1280, height: 720 }, { width: 375, height: 667 }]) {
    await page.setViewportSize(viewport);
    await page.goto(`/dashboard`, { headers: { 'x-tenant-id': tenantA.id } });
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-content"]')).toBeVisible();
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="dashboard-latency"]')).toHaveText(/\d+ms/);
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for real-time collaboration systems must handle offline edits, eventual consistency, and deterministic merges. This question evaluates your ability to architect an end-to-end flow that exercises offline queues, CRDT-based merging, and version history while ensuring test isolation and reproducibility.

## Key Concepts

- Offline-first collaboration with optimistic UI updates
- CRDT-based merge algorithms ensuring deterministic outcomes
- Test data isolation using per-test seeds and unique identifiers
- Server-sync mocks with controlled timing for deterministic test results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Setup two Playwright contexts for concurrent user simulation
const contextA = await browser.newContext();
const contextB = await browser.newContext();
const pageA = await contextA.newPage();
const pageB = await contextB.newPage();

// Seed a shared base document
const docId = await seedDocument({ content: 'Initial paragraph.' });

// Simulate offline for both users
await setNetworkOffline(pageA);
await setNetworkOffline(pageB);

// User A edits the paragraph
await pageA.goto(`/doc/${docId}`);
await pageA.locator('.editor').fill('User A edited this.');

// User B edits the same paragraph offline
await pageB.goto(`/doc/${docId}`);
await pageB.locator('.editor').fill('User B edited that.');

// Reconnect both users to trigger CRDT sync
await setNetworkOnline(pageA);
await setNetworkOnline(pageB);

// Wait for sync completion
await expect(pageA.locator('.editor')).toHaveText('Expected merged content');
await expect(pageB.locator('.editor')).toHaveText('Expected merged content');

// Verify idempotent replay
const versionBefore = await getDocumentVersion(docId);
await triggerSyncAgain(pageA);
const versionAfter = await getDocumentVersion(docId);
expect(versionAfter).toBe(versionBefore);

// Verify version history
const history = await getVersionHistory(docId);
expect(history).toContain('User A edited this.');
expect(history).toContain('User B edited that.');
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for serverless, multi-tenant extension sandboxes require robust isolation guarantees, quota enforcement under burst loads, and reliable event delivery semantics. This question evaluates your ability to architect end-to-end test flows that encompass test data seeding, observability instrumentation, concurrency handling, and comprehensive failure mode validation across tenant boundaries.

## Key Concepts

- **Tenant Isolation**: Strict data separation at database, memory, and network levels
- **Per-Tenant Quotas**: Resource limits enforced under burst conditions
- **Event Delivery Semantics**: At-least-once guarantees with idempotent processing
- **Sandbox Enforcement**: Extension runtime boundaries and permission scoping
- **Audit Trails**: Complete visibility into extension lifecycle and data access
- **Cleanup Verification**: Guaranteed resource teardown after uninstallation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Test fixture: tenant event flow setup
async function seedTenantData(tenantId, db) {
  await db.users.insertMany([
    { id: `u1_${tenantId}`, tenantId, name: 'Admin' },
    { id: `u2_${tenantId}`, tenantId, name: 'Member' }
  ]);
  await db.channels.insertMany([
    { id: `ch1_${tenantId}`, tenantId, name: 'general' }
  ]);
}

async function deployExtension(tenantId, extensionId, permissions) {
  const sandbox = await createIsolatedSandbox({ tenantId, permissions });
  await sandbox.deploy(extensionId);
  return sandbox;
}

async function fireBurstEvents(eventBus, tenants, count) {
  const events = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < count; i++) {
    const tenant = tenants[i % tenants.length];
    events.push(eventBus.publish({
      tenantId: tenant.id,
      type: 'message.created',
      payload: { id: `msg_${i}`, channelId: `ch1_${tenant.id}` }
    }));
  }
  await Promise.all(events);
}

// Validation assertions
async function validateIsolation(db, tenants) {
  for (const tenant of tenants) {
    const otherData = await db.events.find({ tenantId: { $ne: tenant.id } }).toArray();
    const tenantData = await db.events.find({ tenantId: tenant.id }).toArray();

    for (const event of tenantData) {
      expect(event.payload.channelId).toContain(tenant.id);
    }
  }
}

async function validateDelivery(eventBus, expectedCounts) {
  for (const [tenantId, expected] of Object.entries(expectedCounts)) {
    const processed = await eventBus.getProcessedCount(tenantId);
    expect(processed).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(expected);
  }
}

async function validateCleanup(sandbox, db) {
  await sandbox.uninstall();
  const webhooks = await db.webhooks.find({ sandboxId: sandbox.id }).toArray();
  const auditLogs = await db.auditLogs.find({ resourceId: sandbox.id }).toArray();

  expect(webhooks).toHaveLength(0);
  expect(auditLogs).toMatchObject([{ action: 'uninstall', status: 'success' }]);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates a candidate's ability to design deterministic end-to-end tests for complex user flows controlled by remote configuration. It demonstrates understanding of feature flag architecture, test data management, and handling asynchronous state changes. The question also probes awareness of telemetry validation and regression risks when configuration changes mid-session.

## Key Concepts

- **Feature Flags**: Remote configuration controlling feature availability without code deployment.
- **Test Data Seeding**: Creating reproducible user states and regional variants.
- **State Validation**: Verifying correct UI renders based on flag state.
- **Analytics Integration**: Ensuring telemetry events fire accurately per business requirements.
- **Regression Testing**: Confirming mid-session flag changes don't corrupt user state.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for NewUserTour feature flag
describe('NewUserTour Feature Flag', () => {
  let mockFlagService;

  beforeEach(() => {
    mockFlagService = new MockFeatureFlagService();
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', false);
  });

  test('OFF state: user bypasses onboarding', async ({ page }) => {
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-modal')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('ON state: onboarding completes successfully', async ({ page }) => {
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('.onboarding-step-1')).toBeVisible();
    await page.click('[data-testid="next-step"]');
    await expect(page.locator('.onboarding-step-2')).toBeVisible();
    await page.click('[data-testid="complete-onboarding"]');
    await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-modal')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('mid-session toggle: onboarding aborts when flag turns OFF', async ({ page }) => {
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('.onboarding-step-1')).toBeVisible();
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', false);
    await page.reload();
    await expect(page.locator('#onboarding-modal')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('regional variation: EU users see GDPR consent step', async ({ page }) => {
    seedUser({ region: 'EU' });
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('.gdpr-consent-step')).toBeVisible();
  });

  test('analytics: events fire on step completion', async ({ page }) => {
    mockFlagService.setFlag('NewUserTour', true);
    const analytics = new MockAnalytics();
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await page.click('[data-testid="next-step"]');
    expect(analytics.events).toContainEqual(
      expect.objectContaining({ name: 'onboarding_step_completed', step: 1 })
    );
  });
});
```

## Evaluation Criteria

- Correctly mocks the remote flag service
- Covers both ON and OFF paths
- Tests at least one regional variation
- Validates UI state at each step
- Confirms analytics events fire appropriately
- Demonstrates handling of mid-session flag changes

